{
    "title": "Cost of Living Adjustment to Royalty Rates and Terms for Making and Distributing Phonorecords",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 01, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-01/pdf/2025-21695.pdf",
    "summary": "Here\u2019s the gist: federal agencies issued year-end updates that include small, inflation-based increases to certain royalties for 2026, a wording change in Defense Department ID card rules, and the Environmental Protection Agency pulling back a contracting rule after public pushback.\n\nSongwriters and music publishers stand to benefit from a modest bump in royalties for physical music formats and permanent downloads. The rate moves to 13.1 cents per song or 2.52 cents per minute of playing time. Creators who rely on sales of CDs, vinyl, and downloads will see a little more income per track. Copyright owners of TV programming also benefit from a 2.9% increase in fees paid by satellite providers for distant retransmissions, bringing the monthly rates to 37 cents per home subscriber and 76 cents per commercial subscriber.\n\nOn the other side, record labels, distributors, and retailers who manufacture and sell physical music or downloads will pay slightly more per track. Some may pass these costs along, which could mean small price increases for consumers. Satellite TV providers will also face a small fee increase and may choose to reflect that in subscriber bills, including for bars and restaurants. The changes are tied to inflation and are relatively modest in dollar terms, but they can add up across large catalogs and subscriber bases.\n\nThe Defense Department\u2019s rule makes a terminology change in its ID card regulations, replacing the word \u201cgender\u201d with \u201csex\u201d and updating related data fields. The stated aim is to align terms across the system. This could improve administrative consistency, but it may raise concerns for service members and family members who want identification to reflect gender identity rather than sex. The department notes the rule does not have broader effects on state, local, or tribal governments.\n\nFinally, the Environmental Protection Agency withdrew a contracting rule after receiving adverse comments. Those who opposed the change will view this as a win for due process and further review, while supporters of the proposed update will see a delay that keeps current contracting practices in place for now.",
